// machine's reed channel opened in about 7 ms: Li, Almeida, Smith and Wolfe
// 2016, "The effect of blowing pressure, lip force and tonguing on transients:
// a study using a clarinet-playing machine", JASA 140:1089-1100). An accent is
// firmer: players release at a higher mouth pressure, which rises faster and
// then falls steadily, and its sound grows about 1300 dB/s against 800 for a
// normal note (Li, Almeida, Smith and Wolfe 2016, "How clarinettists
// articulate", JASA 139:825-838), so its tongue lets go faster and its burst
// lasts longer. A legato tongue ("da") barely interrupts the breath (Hofmann
// and Goebl 2014, "Production and perception of legato, portato, and staccato
// articulation in saxophone playing", Frontiers in Psychology 5:690), so it
// lets go slowly with little burst. In fast tonguing the stroke is light: a
// saxophonist's tongue stays on the reed about 35 ms at any tempo (Hofmann,
// Goebl, Weilguni, Mayer and Smetana 2012), which leaves a fast note little
// time for a burst. A "ku" stops the air at the palate and leaves the reed
